This is the GitHub repository for PwBayes.
PwBayes enables compuation of the pairwise (PW) likelihood, which is very fast 
for long sequences, along with adjustment weights that attempt to make 
the PW likelihood behave more like the full likelihood. 

PwBayes is a fork of MrBayes version 3.2.7, which can be found here:
https://github.com/NBISweden/MrBayes.git.

The MrBayes web site is available at

    http://mrbayes.net/


To build and install the source code, run the following from a command prompt:

    git clone https://github.com/gmellison/PwBayes.git
    cd PwBayes
    ./configure
    make 

Then the 'pwb' binary will be created in the 'src' subdirectory.

PwBayes can also be compiled with support for multithreading via MPI:
    ./configure --with-mpi=yes
    make

PwBayes simply adds additional features relating to the use of the PW
likelihood to MrBayes, so its usage will be straightforward for anyone 
familiar with MrBayes.

To see the PW features in action, after compiling run: 
    src/pwb -i simulated.nex


Most of the new code is in src/pairwise.c, with header file src/pairwise.h.
Some changes have been made in src/mcmc.c, src/likelihood.c, src/model.c, and 
src/command.c, and new declarations have been made to the declarations 
in src/bayes.h. These changes have been flagged with a comment
`/* ~PW~ */`

This README contains a short introduction the the options available for 
the PW likelihood. The 'examples' subdirectory contains sample 
NEXUS files using the PW likelihood and demonstrating the various 
options. 

The pairwise likelihood is enabled using the command `lset usePairwise=yes;`.
This option is only available for the DNA datatype and General Time 
Reversible (GTR)  and Jukes-Cantor (JC) substitution models. 
The GTR and JC models are set using the commands `lset nst=6` and `lset nst=1`,
respectively. 

Setting `pwweights=1` or `pwweights=2` will use the adjusted 
pairwise likelihood function with weight calculated with the first 
or second order weights described in the paper. The weight is 
calculated before starting the MCMC run, so reasonable values of the 
substitution model parameters are necessary for obtaining a good estimate 
of the weight. If these are known in advance, they can be specified by the 
user with already existing MrBayes functionality, either by fixing the 
parameters using, for example, 

`prset revmatpr=fixed(r1,r2,r3,r4,r5,r6) shapepr=fixed(alpha);`

or by setting the starting values manually with 
`startvals revmat=(r1,r2,r3,r4,r5,r6) alpha=(alpha); `

If these values are not known in advance, a short MCMC run can be used to
estimate them with the full likelihood. To do this, PwBayes includes options
`initsubmod=[yes|no]`, which is 'no' by default, and if set to 'yes' will use
the initial run to estimate the substitution model parameters for the GTR
model.  The `fixrateparm=[yes|no]` option is set to 'yes' by default, and if
the initial run is used, tells PwBayes to either fix the GTR relative rate
parameters parameters during the main MCMC run or continue to sample them. The
alpha parameter is fixed always, as it is not identifiable by the PW
likelihood.  

The `initrungen`, `initsamplefreq`, and `initrunburnin` all take
integer values, and control the length of the initial run, the sample
frequency, and the number of samples to discard as burn-in. They default to 
10000, 50, and 5000, respectively. The samples from the initial run will
be written to file `filename.runi.init.p` for chain i. 
Note that this is only necessary for the GTR substitution model.  
    
The `numdatasplits` tells PwBays how many independent data splits 
to use to estimate the PW weights. The default is 10. The alignment is 
split into `numdatasplits` independent partitions, with sites 
(p - 1) * (nsites/ndatasplits) + (1:(nsites/ndatasplits)) assigned to 
split p (the notation 1:n is shorthand for 1, 2, ..., n ). 


